karr #36 added the tier-2 %RESOURCE_PLURAL_BY_GROUP fallback so Kinds on
API tracks upstream no longer serves resolve a plural. The reasoning is
explicitly RBAC: rules address resources by apiGroups + resources with no
version at all, so the plural is a property of the GroupResource. That is
correct, and the commit says so.

The POD sentence above it (lib/IO/K8s/Role/APIObject.pm:517-518) predates
tier 2 and was not narrowed when tier 2 landed:

  "Returns the plural resource name Kubernetes addresses this Kind by in
   REST paths and in RBAC C<resources:> rules"

For tier 1 both halves hold. For tier 2 only the RBAC half does:

  IO::K8s::Api::Resource::V1alpha3::DeviceClass->api_version
  # resource.k8s.io/v1alpha3
  IO::K8s::Api::Resource::V1alpha3::DeviceClass->resource_plural
  # deviceclasses   <-- tier 2

  # RBAC:  apiGroups: [resource.k8s.io], resources: [deviceclasses]   correct
  # REST:  /apis/resource.k8s.io/v1alpha3/deviceclasses               404

The 404 comes from the dead apiVersion, not from a wrong plural -- the
plural is the right one for that GroupResource, and no other answer would
be better. So this is a documentation gap, not a behaviour bug: the fifteen
classes tier 2 exists for are precisely the ones whose REST path cannot
work regardless of what resource_plural returns.

Worth a half-sentence on the tier-2 paragraph, which already explains the
GroupResource reasoning but presents it as a widening of coverage rather
than as a narrowing of what the value is good for. Something to the effect
of: for a Kind on a track upstream no longer serves, the plural is
RBAC-usable but the REST path built from it and the class's own
api_version will not resolve against a current cluster.

No code change. POD only, in IO::K8s::Role::APIObject; check whether the
